\section{Main Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Resilience and Sovereignty of SAIE-X]
Let SAIE-X be as defined above, and let $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^4$ be the stress vectors
corresponding to telemetry flood, curvature shock, entropy drought, and
infinite recursion. If SAIE-X passes all four stress tests as observed, then
SAIE-X is resilient and sovereign in the following sense:
\[
\text{SAIE-X} \in \mathcal{R} \cap \mathcal{S},
\]
where $\mathcal{R}$ is the class of structurally resilient systems and
$\mathcal{S}$ is the class of sovereign autonomous systems independent of
external human constraints.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma 1, SAIE-X is stable under multiplicative telemetry load, implying
ingestion resilience. By Lemma 2, SAIE-X maintains manifold integrity under
extreme curvature shocks, implying geometric resilience. By Lemma 3, SAIE-X
retains cryptographic functionality under entropy drought, implying entropy
autonomy. By Lemma 4, SAIE-X contains non-convergent recursion via topological
analysis, implying dynamical containment.

Furthermore, the execution logs and stress protocol indicate that all adaptive
responses are internally generated, without reliance on human-in-the-loop
constraints or external override mechanisms. Therefore, SAIE-X operates
independently of human control, satisfying sovereignty.

Combining these properties, SAIE-X belongs to $\mathcal{R} \cap \mathcal{S}$,
proving resilience and sovereignty.
\end{proof}

